---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
import Media from '../../components/Media.astro';
import MediaGrid from '../../components/MediaGrid.astro';
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="UFO Project"
  subtitle="Canceling linear acceleration with centrifugal mechanics for high-G maneuvers"
  tags={[
    { label: "Reinforcement Learning", href: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reinforcement_learning" },
    { label: "Simulation", href: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simulation" },
    { label: "PyTorch", href: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PyTorch" },
  ]}
>
  <section class="story-section">
    <h2>The Idea</h2>
    <p>
      I am building a centrifugal system that cancels linear acceleration using rotational
      mechanics. The long-term applications are aggressive fighter-jet and missile-evasion
      maneuvers where the vehicle can pull extreme G without putting the same sustained load
      on a pilot or payload.
    </p>
    <p>
      The core trick: spin an inner vessel so that centrifugal effects offset a large fraction
      of the outer frame's linear acceleration, keeping the accelerometer (and a hypothetical
      occupant) closer to a tolerable load.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="story-section">
    <h2>2D RL Prototype</h2>
    <p>
      So far I have a 2D reinforcement learning environment and a trained policy that minimizes
      total experienced acceleration. Earth's 1g gravity is baked in as a constant so the model
      does not waste capacity rediscovering it. RL was the fastest way to find a workable
      control policy without hand-deriving every feedback loop; a more robust classical or
      hybrid controller would come later for a real system.
    </p>
    <p>
      In simulation the outer frame can hit up to 10g of linear acceleration (deceleration).
      The policy spins the vessel such that the inner accelerometer settles around
      <strong>1.64g</strong> of sustained force, roughly takeoff or landing feel instead of a
      10g crash pulse.
    </p>

    <!--
    <Media
      src="/assets/projects/ufo/sim-render.png"
